1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,610
TypeScript seven just broke Angular's

2
00:00:02,610 --> 00:00:03,210
compiler.

3
00:00:03,210 --> 00:00:05,450
So Angular is rewriting part of it,

4
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:06,288
in Rust.

5
00:00:06,338 --> 00:00:07,058
Here's why.

6
00:00:07,058 --> 00:00:09,498
TypeScript seven is the native rewrite,

7
00:00:09,498 --> 00:00:10,098
in Go.

8
00:00:10,098 --> 00:00:11,058
Much faster.

9
00:00:11,058 --> 00:00:12,338
But it shipped without the

10
00:00:12,338 --> 00:00:14,059
transformer API.

11
00:00:14,089 --> 00:00:16,409
And Angular's compiler, ngc,

12
00:00:16,409 --> 00:00:18,689
plugged straight into that API,

13
00:00:18,689 --> 00:00:21,129
to turn your decorators and templates into

14
00:00:21,129 --> 00:00:22,202
JavaScript.

15
00:00:22,232 --> 00:00:24,721
So the team built ngp, the Angular

16
00:00:24,721 --> 00:00:25,712
preprocessor.

17
00:00:25,712 --> 00:00:28,312
A Rust front end, built on Oxc,

18
00:00:28,312 --> 00:00:30,392
scans your files in parallel,

19
00:00:30,392 --> 00:00:32,752
finds every component, and resolves its

20
00:00:32,752 --> 00:00:33,680
metadata.

21
00:00:33,710 --> 00:00:34,670
Then it hands off.

22
00:00:34,670 --> 00:00:36,270
Type checking and bundling go to

23
00:00:36,270 --> 00:00:39,496
tools like tsgo, esbuild, or Rolldown.

24
00:00:39,526 --> 00:00:41,726
Early community benchmarks show builds

25
00:00:41,726 --> 00:00:43,525
up to three times faster.

26
00:00:43,555 --> 00:00:44,715
But it's still in beta.

27
00:00:44,715 --> 00:00:47,732
For production, stay on ngc for now.

28
00:00:47,802 --> 00:00:48,402
The lesson?

29
00:00:48,402 --> 00:00:50,242
Your framework is only as fast

30
00:00:50,242 --> 00:00:51,682
as the tools underneath it.

31
00:00:51,682 --> 00:00:54,082
And those tools are going native.

